# Prefab-first components

## No bootstrap UI

Do not build HUD, overlays, popups, or rows with `new GameObject` + `AddComponent` trees, layout math in code, or RuntimePrefabs assembly of children. Create a basic prefab under `Assets/Prefabs/` with the needed GameObjects, RectTransforms, and components. The human will visual-tune it in the Editor.

## Component ownership

Each prefab / overlay / interactive object holds its own MonoBehaviour and `[SerializeField]` refs. Drag children and assets on the prefab. Avoid god classes that Find-and-wire every control. Match / AppRoot may call public methods on those components (`Present`, `Open`, `Dismiss`) but must not invent their hierarchy at runtime.

## Instantiating

Instantiate the prefab as a child and leave RectTransform / LayoutGroup values as authored on the prefab. Do not re-anchor, resize, or re-pad after spawn unless fixing a clear bug the prefab cannot express.

## Exceptions

- Plain Core C# (rules, board, state) stays prefab-free.
- `RuntimePrefabs` may load the canonical `Assets/Prefabs/...` path in the Editor when a serialized ref is missing — not a design path, and never via a Resources twin. See `one-asset-location.mdc`.
- Do not mass-convert old bootstrap HUD outside Overlays; apply this on new work and files we edit. Overlays under `Assets/Prefabs/Overlays` should own a MonoBehaviour.
